\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {P1.1}{\ignorespaces Part~I in order. Each chapter is a prerequisite for the next, and the last two are the ones everything in Parts~II to~V depends on.}}{2}{figure.caption.6}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces A small enterprise network. Access switches connect end devices; a distribution switch aggregates them and routes between VLANs; the router reaches other networks; the firewall polices what leaves. Every chapter in Parts~II to~IV configures one piece of this picture.}}{5}{figure.caption.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The whole switching algorithm. Flooding is not a failure --- it is how the table gets built, and the second frame between the same two hosts is already efficient.}}{17}{figure.caption.8}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {P2.1}{\ignorespaces Part~II. Each chapter adds one capability to the same switched network, and the last two are about proving it works and finding out why it does not.}}{44}{figure.caption.9}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces The whole idea, in one picture. Four ports on one switch become two broadcast domains that cannot reach each other --- and nothing physical changed.}}{46}{figure.caption.10}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces One cable, two VLANs, and no trunk. The phone tags its own traffic and passes the PC's through untouched.}}{51}{figure.caption.11}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.1}{\ignorespaces A trunk is one cable carrying many VLANs. Every VLAN but one is labelled; the unlabelled one is the native VLAN, and a disagreement about it joins two networks silently.}}{56}{figure.caption.12}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.1}{\ignorespaces Bundling is not about speed for one conversation --- it is about spanning tree seeing one link instead of four, and therefore blocking none of them.}}{62}{figure.caption.13}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.1}{\ignorespaces Spanning tree does not remove the cable. It blocks one port, so the physical redundancy is still there and can be brought back automatically when a link fails.}}{68}{figure.caption.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {P3.1}{\ignorespaces Part~III. Static routing and OSPF are two answers to the same question; first Chapter~15 establishes how the router picks between whatever answers it has.}}{85}{figure.caption.15}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.1}{\ignorespaces Link state in one picture. The database is shared and identical; the routing table each router builds from it is its own.}}{98}{figure.caption.16}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19.1}{\ignorespaces The host is configured once and never reconfigured. Failover moves the virtual IP \emph {and} the virtual MAC, which is why nothing on the LAN has to re-learn anything.}}{109}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {P4.1}{\ignorespaces Part~IV. Three chapters make the network usable; four decide who is allowed to use it. Chapter~20 belongs to domain~1 and the rest to domain~4.}}{114}{figure.caption.18}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20.1}{\ignorespaces DORA. Three of the four messages are broadcasts, which is why a router between client and server stops the process dead unless it is configured to relay.}}{116}{figure.caption.19}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21.1}{\ignorespaces Only step~1 is recursive. The host asks one question and waits; the resolver does the walking and caches what it learns for the TTL.}}{124}{figure.caption.20}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.1}{\ignorespaces The same two hosts, four names. \emph {Inside} and \emph {outside} name the host; \emph {local} and \emph {global} name the viewpoint.}}{131}{figure.caption.21}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.1}{\ignorespaces Why placement is part of the design. The same ACL is correct in one place and an outage in the other.}}{147}{figure.caption.22}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25.1}{\ignorespaces Five features, five abuses, one port. The uplink is trusted; everything facing a user is not.}}{153}{figure.caption.23}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26.1}{\ignorespaces Where the tunnel starts decides the model. Site-to-site protects gateway to gateway; remote access protects all the way to the user's device.}}{163}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26.2}{\ignorespaces Transport mode keeps the original IP header. Tunnel mode encrypts it and adds a new one, which is what lets private addresses cross a public network.}}{166}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {P5.1}{\ignorespaces Part~V. The top row completes domain~1; the bottom row is the whole of domain~5, and every topic in it is new to v2.0.}}{171}{figure.caption.26}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27.1}{\ignorespaces The 2.4\,GHz band. Channels 1, 6 and 11 are the only trio that does not overlap; anything between them interferes with two networks instead of sharing one.}}{173}{figure.caption.27}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.1}{\ignorespaces The reachability ladder. Each rung that passes eliminates everything below it; the first rung that fails names the area to investigate.}}{182}{figure.caption.28}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29.1}{\ignorespaces The difference is one layer. A type~1 hypervisor \emph {is} the operating system; a type~2 hypervisor is an application running on one.}}{191}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29.2}{\ignorespaces VM 1 talking to VM 2 never reaches SW1. Everything else does, over a trunk --- a single physical port carrying three VLANs.}}{192}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29.3}{\ignorespaces One layer removed. Containers share the host's kernel, which is why they are small and fast --- and why the isolation between them is weaker.}}{192}{figure.caption.31}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30.1}{\ignorespaces SNMP works in both directions: the manager polls on port 161, and agents report events unprompted to port 162.}}{200}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30.2}{\ignorespaces Every IOS syslog message has the same four parts after the timestamp. The severity is the single digit between the two hyphens.}}{201}{figure.caption.33}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31.1}{\ignorespaces Everything lives on the control node. The devices need only the SSH access they already have.}}{208}{figure.caption.34}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32.1}{\ignorespaces The difference is the loop and who closes it. A generative system hands you an answer; an agentic one acts and checks its own work.}}{216}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {P6.1}{\ignorespaces The twenty-nine topics by their opening verb. Only a fifth of the blueprint asks you to recall anything; the rest asks you to do something or to read something and decide.}}{224}{figure.caption.36}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33.1}{\ignorespaces The loop is between 5 and 6, and it is the point. A hypothesis that does not predict correctly is discarded, not patched.}}{226}{figure.caption.37}%
